  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-93890",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=3,$ $b=11,$ and $c=7.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(3,11)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}11 & = & 3\\cdot 3 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 3 & = & 2\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =11-9 \\\\ 1 & =3-2\\cdot 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3-(11-9) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4+11\\cdot (-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ and\n\n$$\n3\\cdot 4+11\\cdot (-1)=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 4+11\\cdot (-1) & =1 \\\\ 3\\cdot 4\\cdot 7+11\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 7 & =7 \\\\ 3\\cdot 28+11\\cdot (-7) & =7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(28,-7)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n(28+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,-7-\\frac{a}{d}t)=(28+11t,\\,-7-3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $x=28+11t$ is attained when $t=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{x}=28+11\\cdot (-2)=6.\n$$",
